      "title": "Problem: The Mirror Number",
      "topics": [],
      "fastest": true,
      "reverse": true,
      "shortest": true,
      "solution": "using System;\n\nclass Program\n{\n    static int ReverseNumber(int n)\n    {\n        int rev = 0;\n\n        while (n > 0)\n        {\n            rev = rev * 10 + n % 10;\n            n /= 10;\n        }\n\n        return rev;\n    }\n\n    static bool IsPalindrome(int n)\n    {\n        return n == ReverseNumber(n);\n    }\n\n    static void Main()\n    {\n        int N = int.Parse(Console.ReadLine());\n\n        if (IsPalindrome(N))\n            Console.WriteLine($\"No, {N} is a Palindrome\");\n        else\n        {\n            int reversed = ReverseNumber(N);\n            int sum_reversed = N + reversed;\n            if (IsPalindrome(sum_reversed))\n                Console.WriteLine($\"Yes, {N} is a Mirror Number\");\n            else\n                Console.WriteLine($\"No, {sum_reversed} is not a Palindrome\");\n        }\n    }\n}",
      "statement": "A number is called a mirror number if:\n\nReversing its digits produces a number different from the original.\nThe sum of the original number and its reversed number is a palindrome.\nFor example, 56 is a Mirror Number (56 != 65 and 56+65=121 is a palindrome.)\n\nYour task is to determine whether a given number N is a mirror number.",
